# Files whose change is a legal act rather than a code change.
#
# A licence grant is irreversible for code already released under it, and the
# security policy is the promise people report vulnerabilities against — so
# these must never land on an approval from someone who could not make that
# grant.
#
# This file owns itself first: an unowned CODEOWNERS would let a pull request
# edit that entry and exempt its own author in the same change.
#
# `docs/adr/` is the second entry and the reason is different. An ADR is not a
# legal act, but it is the record that makes a change to one of the repository's
# hard constraints legitimate. Those constraints are the three deployables, the
# Admin Worker's lack of a database, the Jobs Worker's lack of identity state,
# the no-rollback migration rule, the no-rebuild rollback rule, the
# no-cryptography law and the no-authentication-bypass law. Changing one is
# allowed; changing one quietly, in the same diff that exploits it, is not. A
# reviewer who is the sole maintainer is exactly the reviewer whose attention
# this needs to be forced onto.
#
# Deliberately nothing else is listed: this repository has one maintainer, and
# every other path is already gated by the required checks (`ci-gate`,
# `analysis-gate`) and the merge queue. Listing more would add review
# requirements a one-person project cannot satisfy without weakening the ones
# that matter.
/.github/CODEOWNERS @johnitvn
/LICENSE            @johnitvn
/SECURITY.md        @johnitvn
/docs/adr/          @johnitvn
